     1	# Pitch 2 — The cell file (Family A)
     2	
     3	**The unit and the file.** For one robot cell, line or vehicle fleet assembled on a factory floor:
     4	the risk assessment, the validation measurements, the technical file and the CE declaration,
     5	delivered per cell at €1,500–3,000 (estimate) against the incumbent's verified €6,000 per simple
     6	machine and €30,000 per assembly (assembled-machines memo). Second line, same tools: the
     7	substantial-modification file for a retrofitted machine.
     8	
     9	**The rule and the date.** Machinery Regulation (EU) 2023/1230 applies from 20 January 2027
    10	(verified); whoever assembles the cell is the manufacturer of a new machine. EN ISO 10218-2:2025
    11	is published (verified). The new Product Liability Directive applies from 9 December 2026
    12	(verified), which is what makes the modification file sellable to workshop owners.
    13	
    14	**Who buys.** Metal, packaging and food firms of ten to a hundred staff that buy a cobot for
    15	machine tending, palletising or welding and install it themselves or through a small integrator;
    16	and Polish- and Ukrainian-owned workshops that retrofit machines. Cobot dealers need the file to
    17	close sales without a full integrator. The incumbents are TÜV-class bodies and the large
    18	integrators, priced above this segment.
    19	
    20	**Why you.** Regulatory literacy: the Machinery Regulation is book 18's ground. Engineering habit:
    21	measurements, documents, the evidence trail. Technical managers in Dutch industry work in English
    22	on this subject; the workshop owners speak Polish. No Dutch exam; the safety courses exist in
    23	English (estimate €3–5k, TÜV or Pilz). Tools: a force-pressure measurement kit, €5–10k (estimate).
    24	
    25	**The precedent.** Cobots and Machinery Safety Ltd, Corby, one engineer, incorporated 10 July 2019,
    26	active (Companies House, verified); Machine Safety Specialists, Ohio, since 1977 (verified). For
    27	the modification line: the Polish "wymagania minimalne" trade, eight firms surfaced.
    28	
    29	**The ninety-day test.** Three pilot cell files at cost through one cobot dealer, on cells already
    30	installed. Under €5,000 including the course and a rented measurement kit.
    31	
    32	**Steps two and three.** Step two: a Dutch-speaking safety engineer on the sales side after ten
    33	paid cells. Step three, confirmed by the review as a step and not an entry: one-application
    34	palletising cells installed and signed (Olympus Technologies, Huddersfield, £65–115k per cell),
    35	or buying a small panel builder, which is pitch 4's logic with a product attached.
    36	
    37	**The AI line.** Every cheaper robot is another cell, and every cell is a file and a site visit.
    38	The regulation adds cybersecurity clauses that need reading, not software.
    39	
    40	**The kill fact.** A dealer or safety body already sells the cell file to SMEs under €3,000, or
    41	Dutch SME technical staff will not buy it in English. The review found dealers doing risk
    42	analysis in-house (Olmia) and one SME using an external expert (Automise, 2021), so the market
    43	exists; the price is the open question. Call two dealers.
    44	
    45	**The number to settle first.** How many cobots are installed in the Netherlands per year, and
    46	what share go in without an integrator. Trade body and dealer figures; not yet fetched.
    47	
    48	**Verdict under the four assumptions.** Ranked second, and first on fit: the edge is exact, the
    49	unit is countable, the date is real. Hands-on, beside a job for the first ten cells, capital
    50	under €20k, courses of days. If the reader will not travel to factory floors, this fails.

